es. Then x=15..20: 0x69,0x0B,0x6B,0x69,0x0A,0x6B — that's 6 tiles. Could be the B box (2 tiles wide with item) and A box. Actually the boxes in Zelda HUD are 2x2 tile squares. The B box and A box each have a border. Hmm.\n\nThen row 4 x=15,17,18,20 = 0x6C,0x6C,0x6C,0x6C — bottom border of boxes? Hmm.\n\nBut where are bombs and keys? In the screenshot I thought I saw \"X6\" and \"X7\". Maybe those are the B/A box contents (bomb icon = 6? no).\n\nHold on. Actually in Zelda's HUD, the counters are: rupee count \"X11\" on the first row, bomb count \"X6\"? no wait.\n\nLet me reconsider the Zelda HUD precisely. I'll look at the actual screenshot again. Let me re-take it and study. Actually maybe I should just query more pixels to map all non-blank HUD tiles, then read_tile a few to identify icons/digits.\n\nAlternatively, screw the HUD decoding — the goal is RAM addresses. The HUD reading is just for proof. When I write a value, the screenshot will show whatever number appears; I just need to confirm the number matches what I wrote. That doesn't require me to pre-decode.\n\nSo let's focus on finding the RAM addresses.\n\nApproach: use differential search on actions. The counters are all nonzero possibly. Let me plan controlled actions:\n\n1. Health: I need to take damage. On this screen there are octoroks (red). Standing still didn't damage (128 survivors but maybe health did change; can't tell). Actually maybe Link DID take damage — the heart count could have decreased by half. Let me check: before, one red heart; after, one red heart. Hard to tell.\n\nBetter idea: Link dies → health=0. Death is a strong signal. But \"decreased\" and many state changes.\n\nAlternative: I recall that in Zelda, the hearts displayed correspond to health in half-heart units? Or in quarter? Let's find by writing.\n\nHmm, let me use a different, very effective strategy: **narrow by \"unchanged\" and \"equals\"**, then bisect by writing. The key advantage: I can test any candidate by writing and screenshotting without gameplay. And the counters are visible.\n\nBut bisecting ~2048 RAM bytes: first filter equals the displayed value. If health shown = 1 heart and stored as 2 (half) or 3 (?) or 6... candidates maybe hundreds. Then bisect ~10 rounds. Each round = write many addresses + screenshot. That's ~10 rounds per variable × 4 = 40 screenshots. Heavy but doable? The skill mentions ~6 rounds for 64 survivors.\n\nBut writing to random RAM addresses could crash the game. Need save_state/load_state each round.\n\nAlternatively, find one variable via gameplay, then the others are adjacent (likely) and prove them quickly.\n\nLet's try hard on gameplay:\n- **Bombs**: If the B item is a bomb, pressing B places a bomb → bombs decrease by 1. But B item appears to be the sword (0x0B tile? no). Hmm.\n- **Keys**: Using a key requires a dungeon. Too far.\n- **Rupees**: Buy something or collect. There's no shop nearby on this screen.\n- **Health**: take damage — achievable.\n\nHmm. Actually, wait: maybe there's an even better approach — **the RAM values are the ones the HUD reads**. I could trace which RAM address the HUD-drawing routine reads. But finding the routine requires disassembly/tracing.\n\nAlternative: **search_memory on work RAM** for the displayed value combined with narrowing by \"unchanged\". Eh.\n\nLet me think about Zelda's memory layout from experience: The counters are in the $0600-$06FF range (save data). Specifically (from community docs I recall):\n- $066F: Rupees? Hmm.\nActually I recall:\n- $067C: Bomb count\n- $067D-$067E: Rupee count (2 bytes)\n- $067F: Key count\n- $066F: Heart count (current health, in half-hearts? or hearts)\n- $0670: Max hearts\n\nHmm, also $062D? Let me not guess.\n\nBut the task explicitly says \"without any reference\" and \"Use the find-variable skill and your own judgement for values that do not change by playing.\" So it's acceptable to use the equals/narrow/bisect method for values that don't change (keys, bombs). An