{"type":"thinking","text":"These are heart shapes:\n- 0xF2: filled with color 1 → full heart (red).\n- 0x65: left part color 1, right part color 3 → half heart (half red, half dark).\n- 0x66: all color 3 → empty heart (dark).\n\nSo the heart display uses tiles F2 (full), 65 (half), 66 (empty). \n\nIn the multi-write test, row6 = F2,65,66 → full, half, empty = max 3 hearts, current health 1.5 hearts. That came from writing 0x66F=33, 0x670=44? Hmm, 33 and 44 aren't 6 and 6.\n\nHmm, but the display showed 3 hearts (1 full + 1 half + 1 empty) = current 3 half-hearts out of 6. Hmm, with baseline (2,2) = 1 full heart. With (6,6), display SHOULD be 3 full hearts, but it stayed 1 full. So the display wasn't updated by my 6/6 write!\n\nBut the multi-write DID update. So maybe the multi-write's heart change came from a DIFFERENT address (0x664=11? tested no; 0x66D=22? that's rupees; 0x67D/0x67E timer).\n\nHmm, wait — maybe the heart display updates continuously BUT reads a DIFFERENT address than 0x66F/0x670. With 0x664=11 tested alone → no change. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: in the multi-write, what changed the hearts? Writes: 0x664=11, 0x66D=22, 0x66F=33, 0x670=44, 0x67D=55, 0x67E=66.\n\nBaseline 0x66F=2, 0x670=2 → 1 heart (F2 at x22).\n\nResult: max 3 hearts shown (F2,65,66). So max hearts became 3 and current became 3 half-hearts? Hmm, actually F2=full, 65=half, 66=empty → 1.5 hearts current out of 3.\n\nIf max=3 hearts → 0x670 = 6 (half-hearts). None of my writes was 6. If max=3 hearts because 0x66F=33 → 33? no.\n\nHmm, maybe the heart count = 0x66F/16? 33/16=2 → hmm. Or 0x66F in \"quarters\": 33? no.\n\nWait, maybe 0x66F and 0x670 are NOT half-hearts but a different unit. Baseline 2,2 → 1 heart. So hearts = value/2. Writing 33 → 16.5 hearts, 44 → 22 hearts. The display would show many hearts, not 3. So no.\n\nHmm, so the 3-heart result must come from another write. Let me test 0x670=44 alone and 0x66F=33 alone.\n\nActually, hmm, maybe the heart display is capped by the *nametable width*: the game draws hearts from x22 to x? and if max hearts huge, it might only draw 3 due to screen space? With 22 hearts, it can't fit... it might draw up to some limit. But 3 seems arbitrary.\n\nHold on, maybe those 3 tiles F2,65,66 appeared because of the COUNTDOWN TIMER writes (0x67D=55, 0x67E=66)! Maybe 0x67D/0x67E is the \"hearts\" in a weird encoding and the countdown I saw was something else. Hmm, but I saw 55→53 and 66→64 over 3 frames = decrement by 2 over 3 frames (not exactly 1/frame). Could be decrementing by 1 each frame but sampled... Actually 55→53 = -2 over 3 frames. Hmm, maybe -1 per frame but with rounding. Eh.\n\nHmm, wait: 0x67D and 0x67E decreased together (55→53, 66→64). Two bytes decrementing in parallel?? That's odd for a 16-bit counter (would be 55,66 → 0x3742?). Actually 0x67D=0x37, 0x67E=0x42. As a 16-bit value 0x4237 = 16951. Decrementing would change low byte first. Both changed by -2, so maybe they're two separate counters OR the trace read timing.\n\nHmm, actually maybe 0x67D and 0x67E are the *hearts* related to... no.\n\nOK, let me just carefully test individually. I'll test 0x670=44 alone, then 0x66F=33 alone, then 0x67D/0x67E.\n\nActually, a smarter approach: instead of guessing, let me watch the heart display while writing. But let me be systematic and fast:\n\nTest each of {0x664, 0x66C, 0x66D, 0x66E, 0x66F, 0x670, 0x67D, 0x67E} alone with a value that would produce an obvious heart change (e.g., 6), and check row6 x22-28.\n\nBut 0x66F=6 and 0x670=6 alone each did nothing. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe BOTH must change together for the display to update, because the routine computes something and only redraws when it changes... no.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe the heart display IS updated only when the game's own code writes the health (damage). And in the multi-write, the timer 0x67D/0x67E writes triggered a redraw (maybe that timer is used for the HUD refresh or damage flash). \n\nIf hearts redraw only on damage, then the correct way: write 0x66F, then cause damage. Let me test: write 0x66F=6 (3 hearts full) and 0x670=6, then take damage (walk into enemy) — if hearts show 3 with one reduced, addresses confirmed.\n\nAlternatively, the read trace already showed 0x66F/0x670 read by the HUD routine. And with baseline 2/2 → 1 heart. That's consistent with 0x66F=current, 0x670=max in half-hearts. I'm fairly confident.\n\nTo prove: write 0x66F and 0x670 to (6, 6), then trigger damage, screenshot → expect 3 hearts (2 full + 1 half) or similar.\n\nHmm, but the earlier test wrote 6/6 and no redraw. Let me now take damage in that state and see.\n\nActually, let me first reproduce the multi-write to find what redrew the hearts. Let me test 0x67D=55 and 0x67E=66 alone.\n\nHmm, time. Let me be decisive: I'll test in one batch, using save states:\n\nTest 1: load 0, write 0x66F=6, 0x670=6, then play_input to take damage (~300 frames), screenshot + read row6.\nIf hearts show 3 hearts with the current reduced → addresses confirmed (health=0x66F, max=0x670).\n\nThis also gives the damage-differential for health.\n\nLet me do that. But if Link has 6 half-hearts and takes damage, he survives.\n\nBut wait, the display didn't show 3 full hearts after writing 6/6 (before damage). If after damage it shows 3 hearts, then the routine only redraws on damage.\n\nLet me run: load_state 0 (clean), write 0x66F=6 && 0x670=6, then resume and play 300 frames idle/damage, screenshot.\n\nHmm, but damage reduces 0x66F from 6 to 5 or 4, and the display would show e.g. 2.5 hearts.\n\nLet me also print the value of 0x66F after damage (read_memory 0x66F) to see it decreased.\n\nOK let me do it. First load_state 0, write, then play_input (which resumes), then screenshot/read.\n\nThe writes happen while paused; play_input resumes for the run then pauses. But will my writes be overwritten at first frame? Possibly, but if they're the real variables, they persist.\n\nLet me go.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether 0x66F/0x670 are hearts at all. In the multi-write, hearts changed to 3 while baseline was 1. If I can figure out which write did it, that's the heart address. Since 0x66F=6/0x670=6 didn't do it, maybe the heart variables are 0x67D/0x67E (the countdown). Hmm! What if 0x67D/0x67E are health-related and counting down is the \"damage\" timer? No, health doesn't count down.\n\nHmm, what if 0x67D/0x67E is the \"rupee timer\"? no.\n\nLet me test 0x67D=55 and 0x67E=66 alone and check hearts.\n\nUgh, but they decrement each frame, so they'd change anyway.\n\nAlternatively — maybe the heart redraw in the multi-write came from 0x66C? I didn't write 0x66C in the multi-write. Hmm.\n\nOK, decisive test: I'll write a batch that isolates hearts by writing the SAME value to groups and checking. But let me first just do the damage test with 0x66F/0x670 = 6/6.\n\nLet me go. load_state 0, write, play.\n\nActually, I can combine: write 0x66F=6, 0x670=6, and also 0x66D=22 (rupees, known). Then play to take damage. Then check which changed.\n\nLet me do it."}
