{"type":"thinking","text":"Now memory 0x66B..0x676: [3, 0, 11, 3, 5, 197, 7, 3, 3, 7, 7, 3] → 0x66B=3, 0x66C=0, 0x66D=11, 0x66E=3, 0x66F=5 (health), 0x670=197 (scratch/palette), 0x671=7, 0x672=3, 0x673=3, 0x674=7, 0x675=7, 0x676=3.\n\nAnd the display still shows ONE heart (0xF2 at x22) even though health=5?! So the heart display does NOT update from 0x66F directly (only max hearts shown, and one heart is full). Hmm. So the number of hearts shown = max hearts = 1? But health=5 (2.5 hearts) → the 1 displayed heart is full.\n\nHmm, so the display shows N hearts where N = max hearts, and fills them according to health. With max=1 heart and health=5, it shows 1 full heart.\n\nSo max hearts must be ≤ 3? Let's figure out: baseline max was 1 heart. So max = 1 heart = 2 half-hearts. Which byte is 2? Hmm, 0x66F was 2 baseline. Maybe the game derives max hearts from... hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the number of hearts displayed is determined by a separate \"max hearts\" byte, and with baseline it's 1. When I did the multi-write, it showed 3 hearts — so something in that batch set max hearts to 3 (or 6 half-hearts). Which write? The candidates in that batch that I haven't isolated: 0x66F=33, 0x670=44, 0x67D=55, 0x67E=66. I tested 0x664=11 alone (no), 0x66F=6/0x670=6 (no).\n\nHmm! What if max hearts = 0x66E? Baseline 0x66E=3 → hmm, 3 hearts? But display showed 1 heart. Unless max hearts byte is 0x66E and value 3 means... hmm. Hmm, but in the multi-write I didn't write 0x66E.\n\nHmm wait, in the multi-write the values written were 0x664=11, 0x66D=22, 0x66F=33, 0x670=44, 0x67D=55, 0x67E=66. And the display showed 3 hearts (F2,65,66 = full, half, empty). That means max=3 hearts and current=1.5 hearts. Hmm, where would max=3 come from? None of the written bytes was 3 or 6. But 0x66E was left at baseline 3! So maybe 0x66E IS max hearts = 3?? But then baseline display should show 3 hearts, not 1.\n\nHmm, unless the display routine shows hearts based on 0x66F (health) only when... ugh.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the display worked like this: hearts displayed = something derived from 0x66F and 0x66E. Baseline 0x66F=2, 0x66E=3. Display: 1 heart. Multi-write: 0x66F=33, 0x66E=3. Display: 3 hearts (F2,65,66).\n\nIf hearts shown = min(ceil(0x66F/2), 0x66E)? Baseline: ceil(2/2)=1, min(1,3)=1 → 1 heart ✓. Multi: ceil(33/2)=17, min(17,3)=3 → 3 hearts ✓. Hmm plausible! So 0x66E = max hearts (containers count), 0x66F = health in half-hearts.\n\nBut wait, the display showed F2,65,66 with 0x66F=33 → 16.5 hearts, capped at 3 (0x66E). And full/half/empty → current 1.5 hearts? If current=33 half-hearts and max=3 hearts (6 half), then all 3 would be full. But display showed 1 full, 1 half, 1 empty = 3 half-hearts current. Hmm, so maybe health was clamped to 3? Or the display formula differs.\n\nHmm, hmm. Actually maybe 0x66F is NOT health in half-hearts. The watch showed 0x66F going 6→5 on damage, which is -1. If damage = half heart, then health is in half-hearts and 0x66F=5 means 2.5 hearts. But max displayed = 1 heart, so the display shows 1 heart full regardless.\n\nHmm, so where does the display get the heart COUNT? Let me test 0x66E directly: write 0x66E=6, 0x66F=6, damage, see if 3 hearts show.\n\nActually the cleanest test: set 0x66E=6 (max) and 0x66F=6 (health), then screenshot. But we saw writing 0x66F alone didn't change display. Let me write 0x66E=6 alone and screenshot.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the max hearts is at 0x66E and the display only redraws when health changes via damage. Ugh.\n\nLet me just test: load_state 0, write 0x66E=6, then play to take damage (which forces redraw), screenshot → if 3 hearts appear, 0x66E=max hearts.\n\nLet me do that. Also write 0x66F=6 to have health.\n\nActually, in the previous run I wrote 0x66F=6 (health) and 0x670=6; the display didn't change before damage. After damage (health 6→5), the display showed 1 heart. So the redraw happened (damage) but showed 1 heart → max=1. So max hearts byte is 1 heart = 2? Hmm, which byte is 2? 0x66E=3 at baseline. 0x66F was 2 (but that's health).\n\nWait — maybe I have it backwards: maybe 0x66F is MAX hearts and health is elsewhere. The watch showed 0x66F 6→5 on damage... that's health. Hmm.\n\nUnless damage decreases a \"health\" byte at 0x66F and max is at 0x66E, and the display = max hearts with filling. Baseline: 0x66F=2 (health=2), 0x66E=3 (max=3 hearts?). Display 1 heart. Hmm, doesn't fit (max 3 should show 3 hearts).\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me look at the save-file format. Actually, maybe the number of heart containers is stored in the SAVE data (SRAM) and only copied to RAM... hmm.\n\nLet me find the heart count via the display. The display shows 1 heart at baseline. If I write a candidate and the display changes to 3 hearts, that's max. Candidates: 0x66E, 0x664, 0x66C...\n\nActually, we KNOW the multi-write produced 3 hearts. Let me bisect the multi-write! I'll replay the multi-write in halves. But careful: 0x670 is scratch and 0x67D/0x67E are a timer. The heart-count change might have come from 0x670 or 0x67D/0x67E transiently affecting the display. Hmm, since 0x670 is scratch used for palette, its value at the moment of redraw could be anything.\n\nHmm, so the 3-heart display in the multi-write might have been a transient glitch from writing the scratch byte 0x670=44. Hmm.\n\nLet me be rigorous. Let me isolate by re-running the multi-write minus one byte at a time. But that's many runs. Alternatively, test the most plausible: 0x66E.\n\nTest: load 0, read baseline (0x66E=3, 0x66F=2), write 0x66E=0, screenshot after 3 frames. If display loses the heart → 0x66E controls heart count. If no change, write 0x66E=6 and see.\n\nWait, the display didn't respond to 0x66F writes without damage, so maybe it won't respond to 0x66E either without an event. Hmm. But maybe 0x66E is \"max hearts\" and the display redraws only on damage.\n\nUgh. Let me test with damage: write 0x66E=0 and 0x66F=6, damage, see.\n\nHmm, but if 0x66E=0, max=0 → no hearts.\n\nLet me instead do a positive test: write 0x66E=6 (3 hearts) and 0x66F=6, damage, screenshot. If 3 hearts appear → 0x66E = max hearts (containers).\n\nLet me run that.\n\nActually, I realize I should also consider that the number of hearts might be capped by what fits and 0x66E=3 baseline means 3 half-hearts? If 0x66E is max in half-hearts = 3 → 1.5 hearts → but display showed 1 heart. Hmm, maybe display truncates: 1 heart. And health 0x66F=2 (1 heart). Hmm.\n\nIf max=3 half-hearts (1.5 hearts), the display might show 1 full heart (if it only draws whole hearts?) Hmm, but we saw half-heart tiles exist.\n\nHmm hmm. Let me test 0x66E=6 (max=3 hearts) & 0x66F=6 (health=3 hearts) and damage.\n\nLet me do it: load 0, write 0x66E=6, 0x66F=6, play 400 frames idle (damage), screenshot + read row6.\n\nIf display shows 3 hearts, then 0x66E=max.\n\nLet me go. I'll also watch 0x66E,0x66F.\n\nActually I already have slot 0 state = clean. Let me load and write.\n\nAlso, I still need bombs and keys. Let me tackle those after hearts.\n\nFor bombs/keys, the read trace showed only 0x66D and 0x66F as counter-like reads in 0x660-0x68F. So bombs/keys are elsewhere. Let me trace reads over 0x600-0x660 and 0x6A0-0x6FF later.\n\nLet me run the heart max test now."}
