{"type":"thinking","text":"Pressing B didn't change 0x66E (3) or 0x0658 (7). So B didn't use a bomb (Link's B item isn't a bomb, or B does nothing). Also note 0x66E=3 and 0x0658=7 are the baseline.\n\nInteresting: 0x0670 baseline is 2 and it got clobbered to 241,225,... again during play. So 0x0670 is being used as a temporary during play. Wait, but this run had no damage (health 0x66F unchanged? not watched). Hmm, 0x670 changed to 241 etc. after ~frame 75. So 0x670 is definitely clobbered during normal play. Hmm. That means 0x670 is NOT the max hearts (or the game uses it as scratch which would be a bug). \n\nHmm, hold on. Maybe 0x670 is used by the *sound engine* or a stack. But the HUD routine reads it as max hearts... Confusing.\n\nWait, maybe 0x670 is genuinely max hearts but the game writes the same address for a \"damage flash palette\" because the HUD max hearts is recomputed each frame from elsewhere. Hmm.\n\nHmm, actually — maybe I mis-identified: maybe 0x66F is not health but something else, and the real health/max are computed. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe routine at 0x6D0D:\n```\nlda 0x066f\nsta 0x0e\nlda 0x0670\nsta 0x0f\njsr 0x6e79\n```\nHmm, maybe 0x66F and 0x670 are \"current hearts pattern\" and \"max\" used for drawing, and the damage routine decrements 0x66F. And 0x670 is also used as scratch elsewhere → maybe the game recomputes 0x670 each frame? But then writing 0x670 should get overwritten, explaining no display change!\n\nYes! That explains it: 0x670 is recomputed/overwritten by the game, so my writes to it are lost. Hmm, but then max hearts must come from somewhere.\n\nHmm, wait: 0x670's watched values during this B-press run: 2,2,...,then 241,225,209,193. Those look like palette animation values (reds). So 0x670 is being used as a palette/color scratch. That cannot be max hearts.\n\nSo maybe the heart routine's 0x0F (=0x670) is actually the \"half heart\" flag computed right before? Hmm, but the code reads 0x0670 directly.\n\nHmm, unless the code I disassembled is not the HUD routine but something else. Let me reconsider: the routine at 0x6CC0 is a copied routine in RAM. It reads 0x066F and 0x0670 and calls 0x6E79 which draws heart tiles. Maybe 0x66F = heart *pattern* byte and 0x670 = ... hmm.\n\nOK, let me find the MAX HEARTS differently. The game clearly displays N hearts. Max hearts is probably stored in the SAVE area and maybe at 0x66E? But 0x66E is a counter (row5). Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the heart display's max comes from a byte we haven't identified. Let me re-read the disassembly of the heart caller and the routine more fully. Let me disassemble 0x6CE0-0x6D20 again and 0x6E79-0x6F00.\n\nActually, hold on! Let me reconsider the heart display formula from the routine:\n- 0x0E = 0x66F (health)\n- 0x0F = 0x670\n\nAt 0x6EAE: `lda 0x0e` (health), if zero → blank.\nAt 0x6EC4: `lda 0x0f` (0x670), if zero → blank; if >= 0x80 → full.\n\nHmm, so 0x670 is used only for the *boundary* heart maybe. And 0x66F's nibbles set thresholds. So the number of hearts might be determined by the buffer/sprite count elsewhere.\n\nHmm. Actually maybe the drawing loop runs a fixed number of times (e.g., 8 hearts) and writes tiles, but only some become visible.\n\nHmm hmm.\n\nLet me look at the caller of the heart routine (0x6D0B) context: it's inside the routine starting at 0x6CC0. But before that, at 0x6CC4 reads 0x0302. And at 0x6CC9 ldy #0x27... The routine at 0x6CC0 seems to be the *rupee animation* plus HUD update.\n\nHmm, I bet 0x6D0B's `ldy #0x03` then heart draw is part of a bigger HUD routine.\n\nLet me look for the max hearts: search for where 0x670 is written. trace_writes on 0x670 during play showed it changing, so the writer PC would be found. But the trace I did was read mode. Let me trace WRITES to 0x670 for 60 frames to find the routine that writes it. That routine is probably the palette flash, confirming it's scratch.\n\nAlternatively, find max hearts by differential: it's a byte that determines the number of